title: Follow nature
lang: en
publishedAt: 2026-11-26
draft: false
summary: "How Apple borrows from the physical world and what the Porsche 911 taught me about keeping what works."
cover: '/images/writing/follow-nature.png'
---
When I want to find a photo on an iPhone, I look for a flower. The old icon was a sunflower. By iOS 7 it had become a ring of coloured petals. On a Galaxy, Samsung’s Gallery icon uses a flower too. Once that shape means photos to you, it can do its job before you read the label. [Early iPhone guide](https://cdsassets.apple.com/live/6GJYWVAV/user/ma616_iphone_ios3_1_user_guide.pdf) · [iOS 7 guide](https://cdsassets.apple.com/live/6GJYWVAV/user/locale/en-gb/ma1681_iphone_ios7_user_guide.pdf) · [Galaxy guide](https://content.syndigo.com/asset/469bbd50-97ab-479c-968c-2da1757f2c57/original.pdf)

I notice that same sense of familiarity in how an iPhone moves. When I say follow nature, I mean looking at how things behave in the physical world. I already know what it feels like to stretch a rubber band or watch something roll to a stop. Apple uses those experiences to tell me what’s happening on a screen.

## Let people carry what they’ve learned

Apple is still the gold standard for me. The Photos flower has lasted through several versions of what a phone is supposed to look like. The drawing changed. What I look for stayed the same. Give people a cue that works often enough and they start looking for it on other devices too.

I see something similar in the face with tears of joy. In the early Google version, the face even had antennae. Look across all three rows and a yellow face with closed eyes, a broad grin and blue tears gradually becomes the shape you expect.

