Watch what someone reaches for before they stop to think. They scan for a shape they recognise. They swipe when they want to see the next picture. Those habits give a designer somewhere to start. Each version is another chance to make the action feel more natural.

## Let the movement carry through

When I reach the bottom of a list on my iPhone, I can still pull it a little farther. The farther I pull, the more it resists. Let go and it springs back. That little bounce tells me I’ve reached the end. [Apple’s scroll view documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/uiscrollview/bounces)

Flick a long list and it keeps moving after your finger leaves the screen. Flick it faster and it goes farther. Then the list eases to a stop. That gradual slowdown gives my eye time to catch where the list is going to land. [Designing Fluid Interfaces](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2018/803/?time=945)

I think about that when I’m designing a card you can drag. While you’re holding it, the card should follow your finger. If you change direction, it should come with you. A spring can carry the speed of your hand into the animation when you let go, so the card has one continuous movement from your finger to where it lands. [Animate with springs](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2023/10158/?time=291)

I look for that same continuity when a design changes over years. One familiar detail helps me recognise the next version, even after a lot has changed.

## Give an idea time to grow

The Porsche 911 has had decades to change its engine, its headlights and nearly everything inside. I can still recognise it from the low nose and the curve of the roof. Put an early one next to a new one and you can see what each generation worked on. You can also see the line they kept coming back to.

{% media-figure %}

![Eight red Porsche 911s line up on a runway, with the oldest in the foreground and newer generations receding behind it.](/images/writing/follow-nature/porsche-911-generations.webp)

{% media-caption %}
Photo from [Porsche’s history of the 911](https://newsroom.porsche.com/en/press-kits/911-s-t/60-years-of-the-Porsche-911.html).
{% /media-caption %}

{% /media-figure %}

That continuity is part of why it feels like a benchmark. You can grow up looking at a picture of one and see a new one years later that still answers to the shape you remember. It takes a lot of individual decisions, made over a long time, to give someone that feeling.

## Find the work you want to return to

I think about this when I wonder what direction to take in my own work. I like figuring out why an interaction feels right. Every project gives me another chance to notice something I missed the last time. I can change tools, try a different approach and still get better at the same thing.

I’d like to do that long enough to make work that someone else uses as a reference. The ambition is to get as good as I can at something I still like doing on an ordinary Tuesday.
